INT. COUNTRY MANOR - SMALL LIBRARY - EVENING

1815. Candlelight. Walnut shelves. A fire in a marble grate. A chessboard stands mid-game on a table between two armchairs.

LORD EDMUND HALLORAN (50s, immaculate, gout-stiffened) sits with a glass of port, one leg propped on a stool. Opposite him, his daughter LADY CATHERINE (20s, plain gown, unreadable) studies the board. Neither has spoken in some time.

She moves a bishop. He looks at it. He moves a pawn. She smiles, almost, and shakes her head.

LORD EDMUND
You have taken a long time to learn this game.

LADY CATHERINE
I had a patient teacher.

LORD EDMUND
I was not patient.

LADY CATHERINE
No. But you were thorough.

He sips. She reaches for a knight and holds it, not placing it.

LORD EDMUND
Your mother would not have approved of the match.

LADY CATHERINE
Mother would not have approved of me playing at all.

LORD EDMUND
True.

She sets the knight down. He studies it for a long time. The fire pops. A clock ticks somewhere in the dark.

The door opens quietly. A tall young man in a mud-spattered riding coat steps in, hat in his hand. This is CAPTAIN JULIAN ASHBY (20s, officer, tired eyes, a faint limp). He waits at the threshold.

LORD EDMUND (CONT'D)
(without turning)
Captain.

ASHBY
My lord. Lady Catherine.

She does not look up. Her hand tightens on a pawn until her knuckles pale.

LORD EDMUND
You were expected a week ago.

ASHBY
The roads were washed out, my lord. And there was a delay.

LORD EDMUND
A delay.

ASHBY
At Waterloo.

The old man sets down his glass. It clinks on the board, and a rook topples. Catherine catches it before it rolls off the table.

LORD EDMUND
Sit.

Ashby crosses the room, wincing, and lowers himself onto a stool. Catherine places the rook back on its square. Her hand is perfectly steady. Her eyes are not.

LADY CATHERINE
Check, I think.

LORD EDMUND
(quietly)
So it is.
